WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
(playful music)

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.760
- [Narrator] This is an economic surveyor.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.800
He's doing something kind of radical.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:06.180
Well, for back then.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.640
He's not asking her about her finances.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.090
He's asking her how she
feels about her finances.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.760
He's surveying for the
Consumer-Sentiment Index,

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.350
one of two main measurements of--

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.850
- Consumer confidence.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.620
- Consumer confidence is very low.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.110
- [Narrator] Consumer
confidence, how people feel

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.590
about the strength of the
economy now and in the future.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.810
Here's how it became
an important indicator

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.570
and why the Federal
Reserve thinks it's the key

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.163
to bringing down inflation.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.510
Consumer confidence is different

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:37.380
from other measurements

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.510
because the man who
created the first survey,

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.660
Dr. George Katona wasn't
just an economist,

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.330
he was also a psychologist.

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:48.390
He began surveying consumers in 1946.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.220
Before that, according to his book,

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.680
economic surveys were
all about asking people

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.500
what their changes in
income were, numbers.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:56.970
But he thought it would be important

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.630
to know how people felt
about their personal finances

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.700
and the changes they expected.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.740
- You have all these
economists walking around

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:07.351
with all their spreadsheets
and all their numbers.

00:01:07.351 --> 00:01:09.720
You have these Fed
officials getting up there

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.290
and speaking a language
nobody really understands.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.400
And then at the end of the day,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.800
so much of all this
comes down to feelings.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.540
- To me, economics is a social science.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:19.650
I am also one of those folks

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:20.970
that's running around with a clipboard

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.650
and looking at spreadsheets.

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.110
- [Narrator] Dr. Joanne
Hsu is Katona's successor

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.550
at the University of Michigan

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:28.080
where she oversees the survey

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:30.120
for the Consumer-Sentiment Index.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.040
- I have always been really interested

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.780
in how people make economic decisions

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.310
and what better way to learn about that

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.630
than just ask people?

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.370
- [Narrator] The survey,
conducted by calling

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:40.710
a nationally representative
sample of people

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.170
on their cell phones asks five questions

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.670
that are nearly identical

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.950
to the five asked 70 years ago.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.240
Compared to a year ago,

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.090
financially, are you
better off or worse off?

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.460
What do you expect a year from now?

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.670
In a year, what do you think
business conditions will be?

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:00.210
In the next five years, do you
expect continuous good times

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.370
or unemployment and depression?

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.070
And when it comes to major
household appliances,

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.500
is now a good time or a bad time to buy?

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.090
- We wanna make sure that our questions

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.360
are timeless and are not really specific

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.790
to the '60s, specific to the 2000s.

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:16.440
- [Narrator] The five
questions are calculated

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.750
into the Consumer-Sentiment Index.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.510
The higher the number, the
better consumers are feeling.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:23.730
The lower the number, the worse they feel.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:25.980
And it's a pretty reliable indicator.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.930
- And if you look at our
consumer confidence measure

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.370
and you look at GDP growth,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:32.670
you can see that consumers
actually do a really good job

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:34.650
anticipating changes in GDP.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.170
A majority of GDP is consumer spending.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:41.340
So what better way to
understand where GDP is going

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.090
than to understand how
consumers are behaving?

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.850
- [Narrator] In the 1960s,
as consumer confidence began

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.040
to be regarded as an important measure,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.410
another survey began, appropriately named

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.390
the Consumer-Confidence Index,

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:56.280
run by the Conference Board.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.200
You can see the results are pretty similar

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.030
to each other but their questions

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:01.770
are slightly different.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.930
The Conference Board
asks more about your job,

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.570
like do you think there
will be more or less jobs

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.760
in your area in six months?

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.650
So their index is more receptive

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.320
to consumers' feelings
around the job market,

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.650
while Michigan's index is
more receptive to inflation.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.500
You can see the difference
in the 2008 recession.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.940
As people lost their jobs,

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:24.000
the confidence index dropped
more than the sentiment index.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.350
- If you focus more on labor markets,

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.030
you're gonna have higher
levels of confidence.

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:29.610
If people are focusing more on inflation,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:32.880
that's something that
will show up on our data.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.550
- [Narrator] In 2022, that is showing up.

00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:36.930
- And what we're discovering now

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.000
with these falling sentiment numbers

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.520
is that even though the
job market is really strong

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.350
and unemployment is really low,

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:47.010
Americans are very
frustrated and pessimistic

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.530
because of the inflation
they're experiencing.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:50.460
- [Narrator] And these indexes

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.080
have become leading indicators

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.390
because they both indicate recessions.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.600
Often when they begin to
drop, a recession follows.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.000
Well, except for this blip here in 2011.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.680
- It was not followed by a recession.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:02.880
Sentiment was extremely low

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.100
because of the debt ceiling negotiations

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:07.650
and that was a factor that
was lifted very quickly.

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.080
- [Narrator] But the
drop in sentiment in 2022

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.610
has economists worried.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:14.250
- It's why the Federal
Reserve is very focused

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.410
on convincing people,
and why you hear people

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:20.040
like Jay Powell say he's
gonna get this under control

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.050
because he doesn't want people

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:25.740
to start thinking that
inflation is here to stay.

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.530
- We think that the
public generally sees us

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.510
as very likely to be successful

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.577
in getting inflation down to 2%,

00:04:32.577 --> 00:04:33.570
and that's critical.

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:35.370
It's absolutely key to the whole thing.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.000
- There is definitely a
potential for self-fulfillment

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.680
when it comes to inflation.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.930
The specific way this typically happens,

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:46.380
if people believe that
inflation is going to get worse,

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.210
they might start stockpiling

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.580
and buying more now if they believe prices

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:51.900
are gonna go up in the future.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.850
And if they front load their consumption

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.800
and bring their future
consumption into the present,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.150
that is an increase in demand

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.910
and that will increase prices by itself.

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:01.320
- [Narrator] It's why the Federal Reserve

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.690
doesn't just need to lower inflation.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:06.540
Chairman Powell needs the
public to feel confident

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:07.590
that they will.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.420
- Jay Powell is playing
a mind game right now.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.210
He's out there to convince
not only the public

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:15.330
but the markets that he's
not gonna allow this thing

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:18.210
to get worse and that he's
gonna get it under control.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:19.440
- [Narrator] The confidence indexes

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.720
can't make consumers more confident

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.310
but at least they provide a measurement

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.630
of how they're feeling.

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:27.630
- Consumers, workers are
the backbone of the economy.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.500
Understanding their intentions

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.930
and their experiences in
the economy tells us a lot

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.060
for the trajectory of the economy.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.010
- [Narrator] Thanks to a
psychologist who understood

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:41.309
that behind all these
economic numbers are people.

00:05:41.309 --> 00:05:43.976
(pensive music)

